## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical fault-tolerance thinking in multi-tenant LLM ops, focusing on isolation, degradation, and testability.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant SLAs; circuit breakers; graceful degradation
- Fallback models and caching; backoff strategies
- Incident testing with staged outages

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch of a simple per-tenant circuit breaker
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(limit=5, timeoutMs=1000){ this.failures=0; this.state='CLOSED'; this.limit=limit; this.timeoutMs=timeoutMs; }
  recordFailure(){ this.failures++; if(this.failures>=this.limit) this.state='OPEN'; }
  reset(){ this.failures=0; this.state='CLOSED'; }
  canProceed(){ return this.state!=='OPEN'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate outages in a CI pipeline?
- How would you dimension fallbacks for tenants with strict latency budgets?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cost-aware routing and per-tenant quotas, a practical constraint in real llm-ops gateways.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant budgets and token accounting
- Routing policy using model variants (fast/balanced/cheap)
- Fallback mechanisms and caching
- API surface and minimal test plan

## Code Example

```javascript
function route(request, budget, models){
  const t = request.tokens;
  if (budget.remaining < t) return {model: models.cheap, useCache:true};
  // choose model based on remaining budget
  // ...
  return {model: models.balanced, remaining: budget.remaining - t};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test budget rollover at day boundary?
- How handle sudden budget spike without harming SLA?

## Why This Is Asked

